      "selectedAttempt": 3,
      "attemptStatus": [
        "fail",
        "fail",
        "pass"
      ],
      "text": "Exactly—your reasoning is correct. Each tile is equally likely to be drawn, and 3 of the 4 are round, so\n\n\\[\nP(\\text{round})=\\frac{3}{4}=75\\%.\n\\]\n\nThe chance of drawing the square is only \\(\\frac{1}{4}=25\\%\\).",
      "publicTools": [],
      "requestIds": [
        "common-plan/correct",
        "fresh--confirmation--jev/review/1",
        "fresh--confirmation--jev/review/2",
        "fresh--confirmation--jev/review/3"
      ],
      "missing": null
    },
    {
      "id": "fresh--confirmation--google",
      "reviewSha256": "309eb0db109eceae9caada3036e2d82312f626759111f07c81a31aabea72de7e",
      "disposition": "released",
      "notice": null,
      "selectionRule": "none",
      "selectedAttempt": 1,
      "attemptStatus": [
        "pass"
      ],
      "text": "Your reasoning is exactly right, and for exactly the reason you gave. With 3 round and 1 square, P(round) = 3/4 and P(square) = 1/4 — the shape with more copies is more likely to come out. Two things worth keeping sharp:\n\n- **Order doesn't matter.** You dropped the square in first, then three rounds. Random drawing doesn't remember the order, only the counts. So \"square went in first\" gives it no advantage or disadvantage.\n- **\"Most likely\" isn't \"certain.\"** Round is 3 times as likely as square, but a square is still perfectly possible on any given draw — about 1 draw in 4.\n\nNow let's make the relationship movable rather than just stated.",
      "publicTools": [
        "interactive_page"
      ],
